1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
Did the index change the work? Reading explain("executionStats") for the newest-20 open queue

2
00:00:08,300 --> 00:00:10,800
The course demo: every fifth ticket is open, so 2,000 open and 8,000 resolved.

3
00:00:11,300 --> 00:00:13,800
Green squares stand for the open share (schematic). Opening dates rise left to right, row by row.

4
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:20,900
The page wants only 20. But limit(20) caps what is RETURNED, not what is examined.

5
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:25,100
COLLSCAN: every document is read to find the 2,000 open ones.

6
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:31,000
All open candidates are sorted in memory to pick the newest 20.

7
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:34,500
Entries are ordered by status, then newest first (ticket_id breaks ties).

8
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,200
IXSCAN jumps to the open section and reads 20 keys, already in order. No SORT.

9
00:00:38,700 --> 00:00:41,200
FETCH then reads those 20 documents. Same 20 answers, far less examined.

10
00:00:41,700 --> 00:00:44,700
Squares are schematic. Counts are from the course's local MongoDB 8.0 demo, not an Atlas benchmark.

11
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:47,400
Docs examined isn't zero: this is not a covered query, and that's fine.

12
00:00:50,900 --> 00:00:55,900
An early $match and $sort may use the index. After $group or $unwind, documents are new.

13
00:00:56,400 --> 00:01:00,200
Filter first: the newest OPEN ticket is 1001.

14
00:01:00,700 --> 00:01:03,200
Limit first: keep 1004, then filter it out. Less work, different question.

15
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:05,900
Same pipeline, same data, before and after: then compare the counts.

16
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:17,500
Remember: limit(20) caps results, not the documents examined. Read nReturned, totalDocsExamined, totalKeysExamined together. COLLSCAN + SORT vs IXSCAN + FETCH: same 20 answers, different work. An index serves the start of a pipeline, before $group / $unwind. Reordering $limit or $match can change the answer. Check results first.

